      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), we start by identifying the standard form of the hyperbola equation and the key parameters.\n\nThe standard form of a hyperbola that opens left and right is:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nComparing this with the given equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), we identify:\n\\[\na^2 = 99 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 36\n\\]\nThus, \\(a = \\sqrt{99} = 3\\sqrt{11}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{36} = 6\\).\n\nFor a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the vertices are located at \\((\\pm a, 0)\\). Therefore, the vertices of this hyperbola are at:\n\\[\n(\\pm 3\\sqrt{11}, 0)\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices is the distance between the points \\((3\\sqrt{11}, 0)\\) and \\((-3\\sqrt{11}, 0)\\). This distance is:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{11} = 6\\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{11}}\n\\]",
